WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.626 position:50% size:92% align:center
The lesson supplies a complete starter program.

00:00:03.626 --> 00:00:06.008 position:50% size:92% align:center
This walkthrough explains how its GPU

00:00:06.008 --> 00:00:07.747 position:50% size:92% align:center
result reaches the Console.

00:00:09.067 --> 00:00:12.980 position:50% size:92% align:center
The GPU writes 42.0, but the CPU prints it. What

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.019 position:50% size:92% align:center
connects those two steps?

00:00:16.339 --> 00:00:20.704 position:50% size:92% align:center
hello is a kernel: a function that runs on the

00:00:20.704 --> 00:00:24.595 position:50% size:92% align:center
GPU. Its body writes 42.0 into output[0].

00:00:25.915 --> 00:00:28.987 position:50% size:92% align:center
The supplied setup reserves space for that value

00:00:28.987 --> 00:00:30.779 position:50% size:92% align:center
in GPU memory. You can leave

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:32.123 position:50% size:92% align:center
this setup unchanged.

00:00:33.443 --> 00:00:36.368 position:50% size:92% align:center
In main, the CPU launches hello. The two ones

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:38.968 position:50% size:92% align:center
request one block containing one thread.

00:00:40.288 --> 00:00:43.690 position:50% size:92% align:center
A thread is one execution of the kernel. An SM,

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:46.876 position:50% size:92% align:center
or streaming multiprocessor, is GPU hardware

00:00:46.876 --> 00:00:49.120 position:50% size:92% align:center
that executes its instructions.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:53.388 position:50% size:92% align:center
The thread writes 42.0 into

00:00:53.388 --> 00:00:56.008 position:50% size:92% align:center
output[0] in GPU memory.

00:00:57.328 --> 00:01:01.465 position:50% size:92% align:center
The program also has a CPU variable named value.

00:01:01.465 --> 00:01:03.792 position:50% size:92% align:center
main initialises it to 0.0.

00:01:05.112 --> 00:01:08.349 position:50% size:92% align:center
Suppose the GPU write and the CPU initialisation

00:01:08.349 --> 00:01:11.384 position:50% size:92% align:center
have both finished. The copy has not started.

00:01:12.704 --> 00:01:17.242 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause here. Is the CPU variable value still 0.0,

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:21.024 position:50% size:92% align:center
or has the GPU write changed it to 42.0?

00:01:26.844 --> 00:01:31.380 position:50% size:92% align:center
value is still 0.0. The GPU wrote to output[0],

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.759 position:50% size:92% align:center
a different location in GPU memory.

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:39.657 position:50% size:92% align:center
The cudaMemcpy call copies the GPU result

00:01:39.657 --> 00:01:41.839 position:50% size:92% align:center
into value in CPU memory.

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:46.235 position:50% size:92% align:center
For this call, main waits for the copy to

00:01:46.235 --> 00:01:48.260 position:50% size:92% align:center
complete before continuing.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.836 position:50% size:92% align:center
value now holds 42.0.

00:01:51.156 --> 00:01:54.896 position:50% size:92% align:center
The CPU reads value and prints 42.0 in Console.

00:01:54.896 --> 00:01:58.239 position:50% size:92% align:center
The kernel itself did not print that line.

00:01:59.559 --> 00:02:03.300 position:50% size:92% align:center
The SM executes the thread. GPU memory holds its

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.405 position:50% size:92% align:center
result. The copy makes that

00:02:05.405 --> 00:02:07.665 position:50% size:92% align:center
result available to CPU code.

00:02:08.985 --> 00:02:12.219 position:50% size:92% align:center
Return to the lesson. Find the assignment inside

00:02:12.219 --> 00:02:13.835 position:50% size:92% align:center
hello, then the copy and

00:02:13.835 --> 00:02:15.452 position:50% size:92% align:center
print calls inside main.
